## Description

The `io.atomix.raft.impl.RaftContext` handles a `MetaStore` object. This object creates and opens a file (see its constructor).  There are tests, like `RandomizedRaftTest` that uses the `ControllableRaftContexts` class, which creates raft contexts, stored in the `raftServers` map.

The `ControllableRaftContexts` creates (or better recreate) `RaftContext`s (which is `AutoClosable`) over and over during the test execution without closing it. That's a problem because it can create over 50'000  `RaftContext` during a single test execution, which translates in the same amount of files (actually can be even more, as a `MetaStore` creates more than one file) being and left open. 

You can check this by adding this method:

```java
    private static void printOpenedFilesByThisProcess(){
        OperatingSystemMXBean os = ManagementFactory.getOperatingSystemMXBean();
        if(os instanceof UnixOperatingSystemMXBean){
            System.out.println("Number of open fd: " + ((UnixOperatingSystemMXBean) os).getOpenFileDescriptorCount());
        }
    }
```

and call it in the `initOperations`. You should see that at every test/property, the amount of file opened increases over time. You can actually see this also between the execution of the same property with the different parameters or just by monitoring the opened file by the PID executing the test using a monitoring tool.

## How the issue has been investigated

Once the error has been understood, I've collected information about the methods which, after called, were leaving a file opened. I achieved this by creating an aspect around all the methods in the `io.atomix` package. This gave me a sort of stack trace of the method that was opening a file, and confirming that the callers were not closing the file. This was enough to start look at the code involved and find the issue (luckily was related to a test class) when putting an entry in the `raftServers` map.

